    <div class="docs-content">
        <section id="getting-started">
            <h2>Getting Started</h2>
            <p>Instella Server stores and serves the releases of applications built with the Instella installer framework.
               Releases are uploaded with the <code>instella</code> CLI, usually from a CI pipeline.</p>

            <h3>Server URL</h3>
            <p>Your server is available at:</p>
            <pre class="code-block">@_serverUrl</pre>
            <p>The CLI requires <code>https</code>. Plain <code>http</code> is accepted only for a loopback host
               (<code>localhost</code>, <code>127.0.0.1</code>) or with <code>--allow-insecure</code>.</p>

            <h3>Before the first upload</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>Create the package on the <a href="/packages">Packages</a> page. Uploads to an unknown package ID are rejected.</li>
                <li>Create an API key (below).</li>
                <li>Generate a publisher signing key and register its public key on the package (see <a href="#signing-keys">Signing Keys</a>).</li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Creating API Keys</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>Navigate to <a href="/api-keys">API Keys</a> in the admin panel</li>
                <li>Enter a name and choose the scope:
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Admin</strong> - all packages</li>
                        <li><strong>Package</strong> - one package only</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li>Tick <strong>Can Upload</strong> for keys that upload or delete versions. <strong>Can Download</strong> is only needed for packages whose download access requires a key.</li>
                <li>Click "Create Key" and copy the key immediately (it won't be shown again)</li>
                <li>Store the key in your CI/CD secrets</li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section id="cli-installation">
            <h2>CLI Installation</h2>
            <p>The CLI is a .NET tool published as <code>instella-cli</code>. It requires the .NET 10 SDK.</p>
            <pre class="code-block">dotnet tool install --global instella-cli
instella --help</pre>
            <p>To update an existing installation, use <code>dotnet tool update --global instella-cli</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="signing-keys">
            <h2>Publisher Signing Keys</h2>
            <p>Every upload carries a release manifest signed with your publisher key (ECDSA P-256). Installed
               applications only apply updates signed by a key compiled into their installer, so the server cannot
               push content you did not sign.</p>

            <h3>1. Generate a key</h3>
            <pre class="code-block">instella keys generate --out publisher.key.pem --password-env INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD</pre>
            <p>This writes the private key (PKCS#8 PEM) and prints the key id and public key. <code>--password-env</code>
               names an environment variable whose value encrypts the key; omit it for an unencrypted key.
               <code>--force</code> overwrites an existing file. Keep the private key out of the repository.
               Generate a second key as an offline backup: if the only trusted key is lost, installations can no longer be updated.</p>
            <p>To print the public key of an existing key again:</p>
            <pre class="code-block">instella keys show --key publisher.key.pem --password-env INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD</pre>

            <h3>2. Put the public key in the installer</h3>
            <pre class="code-block">.WithPublisherKey("&lt;public key from instella keys show&gt;")</pre>
            <p>Add one <code>.WithPublisherKey(...)</code> call per trusted key (for example, the primary and the backup key).</p>

            <h3>3. Register the public key on the package</h3>
            <p>Open the package on the <a href="/packages">Packages</a> page and add the public key under <strong>Publisher keys</strong>.
               Once at least one key is registered, the server rejects uploads that are unsigned or signed by another key.
               With no key registered, signed and unsigned uploads are both accepted, so register the key to catch misconfigured pipelines early.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="cli-commands">
            <h2>CLI Commands Reference</h2>

            <h3>upload</h3>
            <p>Uploads a published directory as one build and signs its release manifest. Files are hashed (SHA-256)
               automatically; files the server already stores are not sent again.</p>
            <pre class="code-block">instella upload \
  --server &lt;server-url&gt; \
  --api-key &lt;key&gt; \
  --package &lt;package-id&gt; \
  --version &lt;version&gt; \
  --path &lt;published-directory&gt; \
  [--os &lt;windows|linux|macos&gt;] \
  [--arch &lt;x64|x86|arm64|arm32&gt;] \
  [--channel &lt;name&gt;] \
  [--changelog &lt;text&gt;] \
  --signing-key &lt;PEM text or file&gt;</pre>

            <table class="options-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Option</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>--server</code>, <code>-s</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Server URL (e.g., https://packages.example.com)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--api-key</code>, <code>-k</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>API key with Can Upload. Defaults to the <code>INSTELLA_API_KEY</code> environment variable.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--package</code>, <code>-p</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Package ID (the package must already exist)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--version</code>, <code>-v</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Numeric version (e.g., 1.2.0)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--path</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>The published files directory (all files and subdirectories are uploaded)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--os</code></td><td>No</td><td>Target OS: windows, linux or macos. Detected from a RID in <code>--path</code> (e.g. <code>publish/win-x64</code>) when omitted.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--arch</code></td><td>No</td><td>Architecture: x64, x86, arm64 or arm32. Detected from a RID in <code>--path</code> when omitted.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--channel</code>, <code>-c</code></td><td>No</td><td>Release channel (default: stable). Any name of 1-32 lowercase letters, digits and '-' that starts and ends with a letter or digit, for example <code>beta</code> or <code>rc</code>; the first upload to a name creates the channel. A version belongs to one channel.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--changelog</code></td><td>No</td><td>Changelog text (Markdown supported)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--signing-key</code></td><td>Yes*</td><td>Publisher private key as PEM text or a PEM file path. Defaults to the <code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY</code> environment variable. An encrypted key's password is read from <code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD</code>.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--unsigned</code></td><td>No</td><td>*Upload without a signature instead. Development only: installers will not accept the release.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>--allow-insecure</code></td><td>No</td><td>Allow a plain-http server URL on a non-loopback host</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>list</h3>
            <p>Lists public packages, or the versions of a package. No API key is needed.</p>
            <pre class="code-block"># List packages
instella list packages --server &lt;server-url&gt;

# List versions of a package
instella list versions --server &lt;server-url&gt; --package &lt;package-id&gt;</pre>

            <h3>delete</h3>
            <p>Deletes a version, including all of its OS/architecture builds. Requires a key with Can Upload.
               Without <code>--yes</code> the command asks for confirmation, and refuses when input is not interactive (CI).</p>
            <pre class="code-block">instella delete \
  --server &lt;server-url&gt; \
  --api-key &lt;key&gt; \
  --package &lt;package-id&gt; \
  --version &lt;version&gt; \
  [--yes]</pre>

            <h3>Exit codes</h3>
            <table class="options-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Success</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Invalid arguments, missing input, or operation refused (e.g. no signing key, http URL, delete not confirmed)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>The server returned an error or could not be reached</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>3</code></td><td>The server rejected the API key (401/403)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section id="ci-integration">
            <h2>CI/CD Integration</h2>

            <div class="snippet-controls">
                <label>
                    <input type="checkbox" @bind="_showPackageSpecific" />
                    Show package-specific example
                </label>
                @if (_showPackageSpecific && _packages.Count > 0)
                {
                    <select @bind="_selectedPackageId" class="package-select">
                        @foreach (var pkg in _packages)
                        {
                            <option value="@pkg.PackageId">@pkg.DisplayName (@pkg.PackageId)</option>
                        }
                    </select>
                }
            </div>

            <p>The examples run on tags such as <code>v1.2.0</code>, publish for linux-x64 and upload the output.
               For Windows, publish with <code>-r win-x64</code> and pass <code>--os windows</code>.</p>

            <h3>GitHub Actions</h3>
            <pre class="code-block">@GetGitHubActionsSnippet()</pre>
            <button class="copy-btn" @onclick="() => CopyToClipboard(GetGitHubActionsSnippet())">Copy</button>

            <h3>Gitea Actions</h3>
            <pre class="code-block">@GetGiteaActionsSnippet()</pre>
            <button class="copy-btn" @onclick="() => CopyToClipboard(GetGiteaActionsSnippet())">Copy</button>

            <h3>Required Secrets</h3>
            <p>Configure these secrets in your CI/CD platform:</p>
            <table class="options-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Secret Name</th><th>Value</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>INSTELLA_SERVER</code></td><td>@_serverUrl</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>INSTELLA_API_KEY</code></td><td>Your API key (from API Keys page), with Can Upload</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY</code></td><td>The full PEM text of the private key from <code>instella keys generate</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD</code></td><td>The key's password (only if the key is encrypted)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </section>

        <section id="troubleshooting">
            <h2>Troubleshooting & FAQ</h2>

            <h3>Common Errors</h3>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>401 "A valid API key is required" (exit code 3)</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> No API key was sent, or the key is wrong, revoked or deleted.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Check <code>--api-key</code> / <code>INSTELLA_API_KEY</code>. Create a new key if needed.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>403 "Key lacks Upload permission for this package" (exit code 3)</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The key is scoped to a different package, or does not have Can Upload. Deleting a version needs Can Upload as well.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use an admin-scoped key, or a key scoped to this package with Can Upload ticked.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>404 "Package '...' not found"</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The package ID doesn't exist on the server.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Create the package on the Packages page before uploading. The ID must match exactly.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"Build already exists for ..."</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> This package already has a build for the same version and OS/architecture.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use a new version number, or delete the existing version first with <code>instella delete</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"Package '...' requires releases signed by a registered publisher key; this upload is unsigned."</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The package has a registered publisher key and the upload used <code>--unsigned</code>.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Upload with <code>--signing-key</code> (or <code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY</code>) set to the matching private key.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"Release rejected: release is signed by unknown key ..."</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The upload was signed, but the key's public key is not registered on the package.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Run <code>instella keys show</code> on the key used in CI and compare the key id with the package's Publisher keys.
                   Register the key, or configure CI with the key that is registered.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"Error: no signing key ..." (exit code 1)</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> Neither <code>--signing-key</code> nor <code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY</code> is set. In CI this usually means the secret is missing or not passed to the step.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Provide the key. <code>--unsigned</code> exists for local development only.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"Signing key '...' is neither PEM text nor an existing file."</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The signing key value is not PEM text and no file exists at that path, often because a secret was truncated or a relative path is wrong.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Store the complete PEM text (including the <code>-----BEGIN</code> and <code>-----END</code> lines) in the secret, or pass an existing file path. For an encrypted key, also set <code>INSTELLA_SIGNING_KEY_PASSWORD</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"server URL '...' uses plain http" (exit code 1)</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> The CLI refuses <code>http</code> URLs to non-loopback hosts, because the API key and packages would travel unencrypted.</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Use the server's <code>https</code> URL. On a trusted network only, pass <code>--allow-insecure</code>.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>"could not detect the target OS from --path. Pass --os."</h4>
                <p><strong>Cause:</strong> <code>--os</code>/<code>--arch</code> were omitted and no path segment looks like a RID (e.g. <code>linux-x64</code>).</p>
                <p><strong>Solution:</strong> Pass <code>--os</code> and <code>--arch</code>, or publish into a directory named after the RID.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Best Practices</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Use package-scoped keys</strong> - Create separate API keys for each package in CI/CD to limit the damage if a key is compromised.</li>
                <li><strong>Register the publisher key on the package</strong> - The server then rejects unsigned or wrongly signed uploads instead of publishing releases that installers will refuse.</li>
                <li><strong>Keep a backup signing key</strong> - Generate it once, keep it offline, and add it to the installer with a second <code>.WithPublisherKey(...)</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Use increasing numeric versions</strong> - MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH (e.g. 1.2.0); each release must be newer than the installed one.</li>
                <li><strong>Include changelogs</strong> - Users appreciate knowing what changed in each release.</li>
                <li><strong>Use channels appropriately</strong> - <code>stable</code> for production, and names you choose (such as <code>beta</code>, <code>rc</code> or <code>nightly</code>) for everything else. An installed app follows one channel.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>FAQ</h3>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>How are delta patches generated?</h4>
                <p>After an upload, the server generates binary patches from the previous build with the same OS/architecture in the same channel.
                   If the patches together are 90% or more of the full build size, they are discarded and clients download the full files.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>What happens if I upload multiple architectures?</h4>
                <p>Run <code>instella upload</code> once per OS/architecture with the same version. Each build is stored separately and clients request the one for their platform.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Can I make packages private?</h4>
                <p>Yes. On the Packages page, set "Download Access" to require an admin key or a package key. Clients then need an API key with Can Download.</p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>

